From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:39:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 042/167] hjviewer: minimum version: the side-loaded Android
 app installs its update

The update screen of a side-loaded Android app has the core download the
new APK into the app's cache (cache/updates), where the core checks its
size and SHA-256 against the signed version policy, and then hands the
file to MainActivity's "hjv_install_apk" (design 5-4). This adds that call
and what the manifest needs for it.

- HjvUpdate.kt takes only a file right in cache/updates. It reads the APK
  with getPackageArchiveInfo and goes on only if the package name is this
  app's and the APK is signed with the certificates of the installed app:
  SigningInfo.apkContentsSigners from Android 9 on, the signatures before,
  compared as sets. Otherwise it deletes the file and answers "mismatch",
  which the screen shows as E4 with a new download. An APK of another
  package would install beside this app as a new one, since Android
  compares signing certificates only within one package. Where Android
  reads no certificates from an archive, the package name decides: Android
  installs an APK of an installed package only when it is signed like it.
- Reading the certificates verifies the signature over the whole file, so
  the check runs on a thread of its own and answers on the main thread.
- Then it opens the system installer: ACTION_VIEW with a content URI of
  HjvUpdateFileProvider and FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION ("ok"). From
  Android 8 on, while the app may not install apps
  (canRequestPackageInstalls), it answers "permission": the screen shows
  A4, opens the app's "Install unknown apps" setting and asks again when
  the app comes back. Everything else answers "failed", Android before 7.0
  too, which takes no content URI; the screen sends it to the browser and
  never calls.
- The manifest declares REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES and the provider
  (authority ${applicationId}.fileprovider; res/xml/hjv_update_paths.xml
  shares cache/updates and nothing else). The provider has a class of its
  own so that the manifest overlay of the Google Play bundle removes
  exactly it, together with the permission: an app from Google Play
  updates through Play only. The installer step resolves the provider
  before it looks at the permission, so a bundle-made APK that was not
  installed by Play fails cleanly instead of sending the user to a setting
  it cannot have.

A build without the update keys never calls; the permission only lists
HJViewer under "Install unknown apps" there.

Known limits: a rotation of the APK signing key would need this check to
accept the APK's certificate history first, in a release before the
rotation; until then the rotated APK is refused as E4. With ACTION_VIEW,
Android may offer another app that opens APK files beside the system
installer; the user picks the installer. If Android clears the cache
between the download and the installer, the screen shows that the
installer did not open until the app is started again, which offers the
download anew. On Android 11, changing the app's "Install unknown apps"
setting ends the app's process (AOSP; still to be seen on a device): back
from the setting, the app starts anew without its download, and the
installer opens after a second download. Kotlin and the manifest are not
built on the development machine; the new Kotlin file compiles against
Android 16's framework classes (Robolectric's android-all) with a stand-in
of androidx.core's FileProvider, and the first Android build and devices
check the rest.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
